arXiv:2509.12440v1 [cs.CL] 15 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking 
<br class="ltx_break">Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jiayi He
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yangmin Huang<sup id="id1" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Qianyun Du<sup id="id2" class="ltx_sup">*</sup>
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Xiangying Zhou
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Zhiyang He, Jiaxue Hu, Xiaodong Tao, Lixian Lai
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The increasing deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) in healthcare necessitates a rigorous evaluation of their factual reliability. However, existing benchmarks are often limited by narrow domains of data, failing to capture the complexity of real-world medical information. To address this critical gap, we introduce MedFact, a new and challenging benchmark for Chinese medical fact-checking. MedFact comprises 2,116 expert-annotated instances curated from diverse real-world texts, spanning 13 medical specialties, 8 fine-grained error types, 4 writing styles, and multiple difficulty levels. Its construction employs a hybrid AI-human framework where iterative expert feedback refines an AI-driven, multi-criteria filtering process, ensuring both high data quality and difficulty.
We conduct a comprehensive evaluation of 20 leading LLMs, benchmarking their performance on veracity classification and error localization against a human expert baseline.
Our results reveal that while models can often determine if a text contains an error, precisely localizing it remains a substantial challenge, with even top-performing models falling short of human performance.
Furthermore, our analysis uncovers a frequent “over-criticism” phenomenon, a tendency for models to misidentify correct information as erroneous, which is exacerbated by advanced reasoning techniques such as multi-agent collaboration and inference-time scaling.
By highlighting these critical challenges for deploying LLMs in medical applications, MedFact provides a robust resource to drive the development of more factually reliable and medically aware models.
<span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Content warning: This paper contains content that may be inappropriate or offensive.</span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="id3" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Xunfei Healthcare Technology Co., Ltd.</p>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">{jyhe14, ymhuang9, qydu, xyzhou30, zyhe, jxhu2, xdtao, lxlai2}@iflytek.com</p>
</div>
  
</div><span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span><sup id="footnotex1.1" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup>Corresponding authors.
<br class="ltx_break">Our data and code: <span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink ltx_url ltx_ref_self">https://github.com/ivy3h/MedFact</span>.
</span></span></span>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Introduction</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, renowned for their versatile capabilities in natural language processing (NLP), are increasingly being applied to real-world medical applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. 2025</a>)</cite>, such as clinical decision support <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rao et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, patient assessment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zack et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, diagnosis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kuroiwa et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, and text classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While various benchmarks exist to assess the medical capabilities of LLMs, they typically focus on tasks like medical question answering (QA)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al. 2021</a>)</cite> and relation extraction<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al. 2020</a>)</cite>. However, the crucial issue of their factual reliability in the medical domain remains underexplored. This gap is particularly alarming given the potential for severe harm from medical misinformation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Togher 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Existing efforts to bridge this gap are limited. For instance, VeriFact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al. 2025</a>)</cite> evaluates fact-checking on synthetic, LLM-generated clinical text. MEDEC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abacha et al. 2025</a>)</cite> is restricted to error detection in clinical notes. By focusing on synthetic data or a single text genre, these benchmarks fail to capture the diversity of medical information encountered in the real world. This narrow focus precludes a comprehensive assessment of LLM fact-checking capabilities and highlights the need for a benchmark covering a broader range of realistic medical content.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To address these limitations, we introduce MedFact, a challenging benchmark for Chinese medical fact-checking, which is built upon three core principles: <span id="Sx1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">rigorous design</span>, <span id="Sx1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">broad coverage</span>, and <span id="Sx1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">uncontaminated evaluation</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rigorously Designed Pipeline:</span> We construct MedFact through a human-in-the-loop pipeline that integrates large-scale AI filtering with fine-grained annotation by medical professionals. We also employ hard-case mining to systematically retain challenging instances designed to probe the limits of current LLMs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Broad and Realistic Coverage:</span> MedFact is curated from diverse real-world texts such as medical encyclopedias. It comprises 2,116 expert-annotated medical texts that span 13 medical specialties, 8 fine-grained error types, 4 writing styles, and multiple difficulty levels.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Uncontaminated Data:</span> MedFact is constructed from proprietary texts under copyright-compliant agreements, making it highly unlikely to be part of the common pre-training corpora for LLMs. This helps ensure a fairer assessment of their true fact-checking capabilities.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We benchmark 20 leading proprietary and open-source LLMs on MedFact across two distinct tasks: veracity classification (VC) and the more demanding error localization (EL). Our experiments reveal that even state-of-the-art models exhibit significant limitations, with performance falling substantially short of human expert levels. Moreover, while retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) can enhance performance, we find that other strategies, such as multi-agent collaboration and inference-time budget forcing, induce an overly critical bias, causing models to misclassify correct texts as incorrect frequently. This finding underscores the critical gap between current LLM capabilities and the requirements for safe deployment in high-stakes medical applications.
Our main contributions are:</p>
<ul id="Sx1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce MedFact, a novel benchmark for Chinese medical fact-checking, distinguished by its rigorous construction, broad coverage of real-world medical content, and its design to mitigate training data contamination.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a comprehensive evaluation of 20 LLMs, revealing a significant performance gap relative to human experts on both veracity classification and the precise localization of factual errors.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct in-depth analyses of model behavior, examining the impact of advanced strategies like multi-agent collaboration and inference-time scaling. Our findings reveal an “over-criticism” phenomenon, and demonstrate that MedFact remains a challenge even for state-of-the-art models using sophisticated inference techniques.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12440v1/pipeline.png" id="Sx1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/201;" width="698" height="201" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An overview of the data construction pipeline and the components of MedFact.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Related Works</h2>

<section id="Sx2.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">LLMs in Medical Domains</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The significant capabilities of Large Language Models (LLMs) across many disciplines have prompted the exploration of their applications in the medical domain <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Medprompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nori et al. 2023</a>)</cite> enhances LLMs’ performance on medical benchmarks by combining self-generated Chain-of-Thought (CoT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, few-shot learning with dynamic example selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2022</a>)</cite>, and self-consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2023b</a>)</cite>. Other developments include HuaTuo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2023a</a>)</cite>, a LLaMA-based model fine-tuned on Chinese medical instruction data; HuatuoGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, a model specialized for medical consultation, fine-tuned on responses distilled from ChatGPT and real-world data; and PediatricsGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al. 2024</a>)</cite>, a model developed via a systematic training pipeline for pediatric and general medical applications.
These advances highlight a growing interest in adapting LLMs to the challenges of medical reasoning and the specialized requirements of real-world practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Benchmarking Medical Knowledge in LLMs</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Numerous benchmarks have been developed to evaluate the medical knowledge of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kweon et al. 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abacha et al. 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. MedQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al. 2021</a>)</cite> is a multiple-choice QA dataset derived from the United States Medical Licensing Examinations to assess the clinical knowledge of LLMs.
More recently, HealthBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arora et al. 2025</a>)</cite> is a benchmark of realistic health conversations evaluated with custom, physician-created rubrics. MedXpertQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zuo et al. 2025</a>)</cite> evaluates expert-level medical knowledge using both textual and multimodal tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">While some benchmarks for medical fact-checking exist, they often focus on narrow contexts.
VeriFact <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chung et al. 2025</a>)</cite> verifies synthetic text against structured Electronic Health Records using a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) and LLM-as-a-Judge paradigm. MEDEC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Abacha et al. 2025</a>)</cite> provides a benchmark for error detection and correction, but is confined to clinical notes. Relying on synthetic data or a single text genre, these benchmarks do not capture the diverse contexts of medical information encountered in the real world, limiting their utility for assessing how well models generalize.
Consequently, a research gap remains for a benchmark that can robustly evaluate fact-checking across a wide array of realistic medical texts.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Benchmarking Factuality in LLMs</h3>

<div id="Sx2.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent research has produced several benchmarks to evaluate factuality in LLMs. For instance, HaluEval <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. 2023</a>)</cite> focuses on hallucination detection through a large-scale collection of automatically generated and human-annotated examples. SimpleQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al. 2024</a>)</cite> measures short-form factuality using single-answer questions adversarially collected to challenge state-of-the-art models like GPT-4.
While these benchmarks provide valuable tools for assessing general-domain factuality, they lack the domain-specific nuances required for high-stakes fields like medicine, where misinformation can have severe public health consequences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang and Togher 2024</a>)</cite>. Addressing this challenge requires a solution that bridges two critical gaps: the lack of domain-specific context in general factuality benchmarks and the lack of real-world diversity in existing medical fact-checking benchmarks. To this end, we introduce MedFact, a new benchmark explicitly designed to evaluate the fact-checking capabilities of LLMs on a comprehensive and diverse collection of realistic medical texts.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">MedFact</h2>

<section id="Sx3.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data Collection</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The construction pipeline for MedFact is illustrated in Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. We began with a corpus of 27,116 medical texts secured through a copyright-compliant data-sharing agreement with a commercial partner. The source materials are primarily drawn from the partner’s internal medical encyclopedia, supplemented with content from its medical consultant platforms, including question-answering pages and user forum discussions. As these sources have not been previously released to the public, this approach substantially mitigates the risk of training data contamination.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data Curation</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-criteria Filtering</span>
We defined a suite of seven leading LLMs: GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2024b</a>)</cite>, GPT-4.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2025a</a>)</cite>, o1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI 2024c</a>)</cite>, Gemini 2.5 Pro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepMind 2025</a>)</cite>, DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI 2025a</a>)</cite>, Claude 3.7 Sonnet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anthropic 2025</a>)</cite>, and Doubao-Seed-1.6-thinking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">ByteDance 2025</a>)</cite>. This model suite formed an ensemble that, via majority vote, filtered the initial corpus by discarding texts identified as <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">overly simplistic</span> (e.g., lacking medical knowledge), <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">esoteric</span> (e.g., highly specialized content), or <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">malformed</span> (e.g., missing a clear subject or context).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This automated process was refined through a three-round, iterative <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">human-in-the-loop</span> feedback mechanism inspired by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al. (2024)</a></cite>. In each round, medical experts reviewed a 5% sample of filtered data. We then used their annotations on misclassified items to construct a more effective retrieval-augmented, few-shot prompt for the subsequent round. This prompt leveraged criterion-specific retrieval, augmenting each input with the most semantically similar expert-annotated example for each of our three filtering criteria: simplistic, esoteric, and malformed.
Over three rounds, the data acceptance rate decreased from 67.69% to 37.00%, and finally to 23.62%. The resulting filter achieved 96.40% agreement with expert judgments, reducing the candidate pool from 27,116 to 6,405 texts.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Human Annotation</span>
The filtered corpus was then annotated by a team of three fully licensed medical professionals. The annotation for each text comprised: (a) a <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">binary veracity judgment</span> (correct/incorrect); (b) for incorrect texts, <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the precise error span</span>; and (c) a <span id="Sx3.SSx2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">suggested correction</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Quality Enhancement and Refinement</span>
We then applied a series of automated techniques to enhance the dataset’s quality and difficulty, reducing the number of instances to 2,862. These steps included:</p>
<ul id="Sx3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="Sx3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hard-case Mining:</span> To increase the benchmark’s ability to differentiate among competent models, we evaluated all annotated instances with our model suite and removed those that all seven models classified correctly, thereby filtering for more challenging cases.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Similarity Filtering:</span> To promote topical and semantic diversity, we computed sentence embeddings for all texts using all-mpnet-base-v2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reimers and Gurevych 2019</a>)</cite>. For any pair of texts with a cosine similarity score exceeding a threshold of 0.9, we retained only one instance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="Sx3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="Sx3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Augmentation:</span> To enhance linguistic diversity and ensure privacy, we prompted a randomly selected model from our suite to paraphrase and de-identify each text under two strict constraints: (a) preserving the original medical content; and (b) removing all Personally Identifiable Information (PII).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final Human Review</span>
In the final stage, the dataset underwent a comprehensive review by medical professionals. They verified that: (a) automated paraphrasing did not introduce errors or alter the original medical intent; (b) all PII was successfully removed; and (c) annotations remained accurate for the paraphrased texts. Any text failing these checks was either corrected or discarded. This process resulted in the final MedFact of 2,116 instances, which was balanced to contain an equal number of correct and incorrect texts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12440v1/example.png" id="Sx3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/212;" width="698" height="212" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>An example of fact-checking performed by Claude 3.7 Sonnet. The medical error is in red, and key steps in the model’s reasoning are in blue. The original Chinese text and reasoning are presented first, followed by their English translations.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="Sx3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.12440v1/figures.png" id="Sx3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/393;" width="698" height="393" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>Distribution of the medical specialties in MedFact.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="Sx3.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data Statistics &amp; Analysis</h3>

<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview</span>
MedFact contains 2,116 expert-annotated medical texts curated from real-world data. The dataset comprises 1,058 medically correct texts and 1,058 texts with a single factual error. It was constructed using a hybrid AI-human framework that combines large-scale multi-criteria filtering, hard-case mining, similarity filtering, and LLM-based augmentation with human expert review to ensure <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">high data quality</span>, <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">diversity</span>, and <span id="Sx3.SSx3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">difficulty</span>.
Spanning 13 medical specialties, instances in MedFact are categorized by 8 fine-grained error types and multiple difficulty levels to facilitate a comprehensive evaluation of LLM fact-checking capabilities. Figure <a href="#Sx3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Data Curation ‣ MedFact ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> presents a sample instance; additional examples are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Diversity</span>
MedFact covers 13 medical specialties, with a distribution led by Internal Medicine (1,156 instances), followed by Obstetrics and Gynecology (240), Surgery (231), and ten other fields. These are grouped into four high-level domains: Internal Medicine and Chronic Care; Surgical and Procedural Care; Specialized Clinical Fields; and Public and Mental Health (Figure <a href="#Sx3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Data Curation ‣ MedFact ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). This distribution reflects a realistic range of medical topics, consistent with the distribution of 27,116 original medical texts, enabling robust evaluation of model performance across diverse clinical contexts.
Beyond its topical breadth, MedFact exhibits significant stylistic diversity. The corpus is drawn from a variety of writing styles, including formal encyclopedia articles rich in professional terminology, popular science journalism, user-generated web content from both peer-to-peer and patient-physician Q&amp;A, and fabricated or sensationalized misinformation. This diversity in topic and writing style ensures that MedFact mirrors the complexity of real-world medical information, facilitating a robust evaluation of an LLM’s generalizable fact-checking capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Error Taxonomy</span>
Developed in consultation with medical professionals, our error taxonomy for MedFact distinguishes between two primary categories: medical and non-medical errors. Medical errors are subdivided into Conceptual, Terminological, Temporal, and Other Medical Errors, while non-medical errors are classified as Citation and Sourcing, Biased Content, General Factual, and Other Non-Medical Errors. Detailed definitions for each subcategory are available in the Appendix. As shown in Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the distribution across the 1,058 incorrect instances is heavily skewed toward medical errors (89.41%), with Conceptual errors being the most frequent subtype (52.65% of all incorrect instances). The prevalence of these medically grounded errors underscores that successful performance on this benchmark requires a deep understanding of medical principles.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx3.SSx3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.SSx3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx3.SSx3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Difficulty Grading</span>
Instances in MedFact are stratified into five difficulty levels. This stratification is based on the aggregated success rate of our model suite on the error localization task (metrics are detailed in the Experimental Setup Section). As shown in Figure <a href="#Sx1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ Introduction ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the resulting distribution across these levels is relatively balanced. This ensures that MedFact contains sufficient examples at varying levels of difficulty, enabling fine-grained differentiation of performance across a broad spectrum of model capabilities.</p>
</div>
