</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Main Results</h3>

<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overall Performance</span>
Table <a href="#Sx3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Data Statistics &amp; Analysis ‣ MedFact ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the performance of all evaluated models. We report the F1 score for VC and Precision, Recall, and F1 for EL.
Among the models evaluated, proprietary models consistently outperform their open-source counterparts across both tasks and all prompting strategies. Doubao-Seed-1.6-thinking emerges as the top-performing general-purpose model, while XiaoYi leads among medically specialized models. A significant performance gap remains between the best-performing models and human experts. The top model F1 score on the EL task, 0.6858, achieved by XiaoYi with CoT, is still below the human baseline of 0.7012. This disparity underscores both the difficulty of MedFact and the substantial challenge for current LLMs. The full results are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A comparison between the tasks reveals a consistent performance gap, with all models performing worse on EL than on VC. For instance, the F1 scores for DeepSeek-V3 and Qwen2.5-Max decrease from 0.6947 and 0.7352 on VC to 0.5380 and 0.6116 on EL, respectively. This suggests that while models can often determine if a text contains an error, they struggle to pinpoint where it is. This difficulty often arises from a “<span id="Sx4.SSx2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">correct-for-the-wrong-reason</span>” phenomenon: a model may correctly classify a text as incorrect (a successful VC judgment) but do so by misidentifying a factually sound statement as the source of the error. Such behavior, rooted in a lack of deep medical knowledge, demonstrates that the EL task is a more stringent measure of a model’s fine-grained understanding and reveals fundamental deficiencies in its underlying medical knowledge.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx4.SSx2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of Advanced Reasoning Strategies</span>
The results of DeepSeek-R1 and XiaoYi with advanced reasoning strategies against a zero-shot baseline are presented in Table <a href="#A4.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Full Result Tables ‣ Appendix D Complete Experimental Results ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. The retrieval corpus for RAG consists of the 6,405 expert-annotated source texts from our data curation process, excluding any rewrites.
Results show that RAG using either one or three retrieved references yields substantial performance gains on both the VC and EL tasks.
This finding shows that providing models with direct access to external, domain-specific knowledge is a highly effective method for medical fact-checking. MedPrompt delivers modest improvements, demonstrating that performance can also be enhanced solely through prompting techniques such as few-shot learning and self-consistency.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx4.SSx2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the multi-agent frameworks (MAD and MDAgents) exhibit a consistent trade-off: they improve Recall at the expense of Precision, resulting in a stable F1 score for MAD and only a marginal improvement for MDAgents. We attribute this pattern to a phenomenon we term “<span id="Sx4.SSx2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">over-criticism</span>”. The collaborative process, which encourages the generation of multiple perspectives and error hypotheses, appears to increase the models’ propensity to flag errors where none exist, thereby elevating the false positive rate and lowering Precision. The slight performance edge of MDAgents may be attributed to its use of medical role-play, which could temper this critical tendency. This finding reveals a critical challenge: while these methods are designed to improve reasoning, they can inadvertently encourage an overly skeptical posture. This undermines reliability in medical domains, where correctly identifying accurate information is as crucial as flagging falsehoods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<table id="Sx4.T3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="Sx4.T3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx4.T3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="Sx4.T3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model</span></th>
