</section>
<section id="Sx5.SSx4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data Contamination</h3>

<div id="Sx5.SSx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.SSx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To investigate potential data contamination within the pre-training corpora of the evaluated LLMs, we devised a text completion task. For each instance in MedFact, we prompted the models with the first half of the text and tasked them with generating the continuation. We then measured the textual overlap between the generated output and the ground-truth second half using ROUGE-F1 scores, where a high score would suggest memorization.
As shown in Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Full Result Tables ‣ Appendix D Complete Experimental Results ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, all evaluated models, even those that achieve state-of-the-art performance on our tasks, exhibit negligible ROUGE scores. This result strongly indicates that MedFact serves as a valid and previously unseen benchmark for these models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Conclusion</h2>

<div id="Sx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce MedFact, a new benchmark for evaluating the fact-checking abilities of LLMs in Chinese medical texts. Constructed via a meticulous hybrid AI-human pipeline, our dataset is diverse, realistic, and uncontaminated, allowing it to probe for nuanced medical understanding.
Our comprehensive evaluation of 20 leading LLMs reveals a critical performance deficit compared to human experts, particularly on the fine-grained task of error localization.
Furthermore, LLMs exhibit an “over-criticism” failure mode that significantly degrades their precision.
The challenges highlighted underscore the substantial work required to develop LLMs that are not just knowledgeable but also factually reliable enough for safe deployment in medical applications.</p>
</div>
</section>
