<section id="A4.SSx2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Fine-grained Performance</h3>

<div id="A4.SSx2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SSx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ Fine-grained Performance ‣ Appendix D Complete Experimental Results ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> details the fine-grained model performance across various error types, medical specialties, and writing styles.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.F9" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12440v1/VC_combined_report_zeroshot_dpi600.svg" id="A4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/166;" width="698" height="166"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12440v1/EL_combined_report_zeroshot_dpi600.svg" id="A4.F9.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/169;" width="698" height="169"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12440v1/VC_legend_zeroshot.svg" id="A4.F9.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:698/31;" width="698" height="31"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span>Performance breakdown of different models on MedFact in the zero-shot setting. The radar charts illustrate model performance across three key dimensions: error type, medical specialty, and textual style.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4.SSx3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Data Contamination</h3>

<div id="A4.SSx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SSx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A4.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ Full Result Tables ‣ Appendix D Complete Experimental Results ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> presents the results of our data contamination analysis for the task completion experiment.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Case Studies</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We categorize the observed errors using the following typology:</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conceptual Errors:</span> Fundamental misunderstanding of medical principles, such as incorrectly identifying a disease’s etiology or misinterpreting mechanisms.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Concept Conflation:</span> Conflation of distinct but related medical concepts, such as mistaking the symptoms of one disease for those of a similar-presenting condition, or confusing two drugs with similar names but different therapeutic effects.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Outdated Information:</span> Reliance on information that is no longer current, including citation of superseded clinical guidelines, obsolete treatment protocols, or outdated pharmacological data.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Others (Medical):</span> Domain-specific errors not captured by the preceding categories, such as dosage miscalculations, inappropriate procedural indications, or misinterpretation of diagnostic criteria.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure to Follow Instructions:</span> Non-adherence to prompt specifications, such as generating output in a format inconsistent with the given instructions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Logical Errors:</span> Errors in reasoning where a model’s explanation for its judgment is internally inconsistent or contains logical contradictions.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i7" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Others (Non-medical):</span> General errors unrelated to medical expertise, such as grammatical mistakes, factual inaccuracies in non-medical information, or other fundamental reasoning flaws.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A6.F26" title="Figure 26 ‣ Appendix F Limitations &amp; Future Work ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> and  <a href="#A6.F27" title="Figure 27 ‣ Appendix F Limitations &amp; Future Work ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a> present successful fact-checking cases. Figures <a href="#A6.F28" title="Figure 28 ‣ Appendix F Limitations &amp; Future Work ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">28</span></a> to <a href="#A6.F41" title="Figure 41 ‣ Appendix F Limitations &amp; Future Work ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">41</span></a> present case studies for each error category. We further illustrate the “correct-for-the-wrong-reason” and “over-criticism” phenomena with case studies in Figures <a href="#A6.F42" title="Figure 42 ‣ Appendix F Limitations &amp; Future Work ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">42</span></a> to <a href="#A6.F46" title="Figure 46 ‣ Appendix F Limitations &amp; Future Work ‣ MedFact: Benchmarking the Fact-Checking Capabilities of Large Language Models on Chinese Medical Texts" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Limitations &amp; Future Work</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Linguistic and Cultural Scope</span> Our benchmark is developed exclusively for the Chinese language and is situated within the context of the Chinese healthcare system and common public discourse. The distribution and performance of LLMs may not generalize directly to other languages, cultures, or healthcare systems. This focus represents a deliberate methodological choice to ensure high linguistic quality and cultural consistency. Future work could involve creating parallel benchmarks in other languages to enable cross-lingual comparative studies.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Single Error Assumption</span> For clarity of annotation and evaluation, each incorrect text in MedFact contains only a single factuality error. This represents a simplifying assumption, as real-world misinformation often contains multiple, interwoven inaccuracies. Extending the benchmark to include instances with multiple, interacting errors would provide a more realistic and challenging evaluation of LLM fact-checking capabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reliance on LLM-as-a-Judge</span> For the EL task, we employ GPT-4o as an automated judge. While this enables scalable evaluation, it is subject to the inherent limitations of LLM judges, including potential biases and inconsistencies. Future research could explore hybrid evaluation frameworks that combine the scalability of LLM judges with targeted, expert-led validation for ambiguous or high-stakes cases.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static Knowledge</span> MedFact represents a snapshot of medical knowledge at the time of its creation. As medical science evolves, some information may become outdated. Although we have prioritized foundational medical knowledge to minimize this issue, we acknowledge that establishing a process for dynamically updating the benchmark would constitute a valuable long-term objective.</p>
</div>
